\subsection{The open accounts}
\label{app:open-items}

The open items are the phenomenographic data: the students' own
accounts of what the terminal is, of what happens to a line of plain
English typed at the prompt, of getting a file into place, of a
pipeline, of a remote shell, and of what a listing shows after logging
in.
Every student writes about the same stimulus, so the accounts are
comparable across students and between the start and the end of the
module; they are read for qualitatively different ways of experiencing
the phenomenon, sorted into categories of description and ordered into
an outcome space (\cref{sec:instruments}).
The stems present a concrete situation and ask for the student's own
account; none of them names an aspect, for the same reason as the closed
items below \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}: a stem that says \enquote{working
directory} has already done the discerning.
The closing invitation---\enquote{if you do not know the commands,
describe in words}---matters at the start of the module, where many
students have never typed a command: an account in words of what they
would want to happen is exactly the data we are after, and without the
invitation those students would leave the box empty.

The open items come \emph{before} the closed items.
The closed items' distractors spell out the candidate conceptions in
full---\enquote{the shell is now attached to that machine},
\enquote{report first saves its output in a file called wc}---so a
student who has just read a dozen such items has been handed the outcome
space, and an account written afterwards echoes the alternatives rather
than the student's own way of seeing.
The other direction is harmless: an open stem shows only a situation,
and whatever small effect writing about it has on the closed answers is
the same in both administrations, so the pre/post gain is unaffected.
This is why the quizzes show one item at a time without backtracking
(\cref{app:quiz}).

<<open items>>=
<<item: what the terminal is>>,
<<item: what happens to this line>>,
<<item: getting a file into place>>,
<<item: explaining a command line>>,
<<item: after ssh>>,
<<item: which computer answers, in words>>
@

All open items carry no points and no correct answer; Canvas's essay
interaction needs only the flag that turns off the rich-text editor (a
plain box invites plain prose) and the word limit.
The items occupy positions 3--8, leaving position 2 free for a further
opening item, just as the knowledge items leave room below 11.

The first item asks for the student's conception of the terminal itself
and of what happens when a command is entered.
It is the counterpart of the question \enquote{what does the word Unix
mean to you?} in the only earlier phenomenographic study of Unix
\autocite{DoyleLister2006Unix}, rephrased for a terminal the student may
never have opened.
The stem says \enquote{type something}, not \enquote{type a command}:
the word \emph{command} already names what the first aspect of
\cref{sec:shell} asks the student to discern, that the line is an
instruction to be parsed, and a stem that supplies the word would be
doing the discerning \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

