The third item is a file-system task told in words: a file in one
place, wanted in another, and a check that it arrived.
It is a task rather than a question because of what the file system is
to the learner: a spatial skill (\cref{sec:filesystem}), and a spatial
skill shows in how one moves, not in how one defines.
The only earlier study asked what the student understood by the term
\enquote{file system} \autocite{DoyleLister2006Unix}, which elicits
remembered words; at the start of the module most students have none,
but every one of them has moved a file.
The task makes the student move through the tree---from the home
folder into Downloads, across to its neighbour, and back to check---and
name things on the way, so whatever they write---commands, a description
of dragging the file in the file manager, a question back---shows how
they picture places, names and the shell's standpoint: whether Downloads
is a place with a path or a list in a window, whether the file keeps its
name on arrival, whether the check is made from somewhere.
The stem names folders and their neighbourhood but no path, no slash
and no \enquote{directory}: the spatial words are the student's to
supply, or not \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

