The fourth item shows a pipeline and its output and asks the student to
explain how the number came out.
The stem glosses the two programs, so that not knowing [[grep]] does not
stop the account; what we want is the student's picture of what goes on
between the two programs, which is where the conceptions of
\cref{sec:composition}---the pipe as a file, as a sequence, as a
stream---show themselves unprompted.

<<item: explaining a command line>>=
{
  "position": 6,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Explaining a command line",
    "item_body": "<p>A student runs the line below. grep prints the lines of log.txt that contain ERROR, and wc -l counts lines.</p><pre>$ grep ERROR log.txt | wc -l\n42</pre><p>Explain, in your own words, what happens when this line runs and how the number 42 comes out. What do you picture going on between grep and wc?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "essay",
    "interaction_data": {"rce": false, "word_limit_enabled": false},
    "scoring_data": {"value": ""},
    "scoring_algorithm": "None",
    <<outcome space: explaining a command line>>
  }
}
@

The gloss does teach two command names, and one may ask whether that
spoils anything.
It does not.
No item depends on the student not knowing [[grep]] or [[wc]]: the
closed items that use [[wc]] (\cref{app:knowledge-items}) gloss it in
their own stems, since what they test is the pipe, not the program.
Nor does the gloss hand over the account: it describes each program on
its own---[[grep]] prints lines, [[wc -l]] counts lines---and says
nothing about what passes between them, which is the question; the
conceptions of \cref{sec:composition} stay open, and a student may still
have [[grep]] save its lines in a file for [[wc]], or the two programs
run one after the other.
If anything the gloss sharpens the question: [[grep]] is said to print,
yet the screen shows only [[42]], so the account has to say where the
lines went---the gap between the gloss and the output is the pipe.
What the student takes away is two names, not an aspect, and the same
gloss is read at both administrations, so the gain is untouched; without
it, the many students who have never seen [[grep]] at the start of the
module would write nothing, and the account is what we are after.

The fifth item describes logging in to a server and asks what has
happened, what the student pictures when typing commands there, and how
they would get at results produced there from the laptop.
The last question is the round trip of \cref{sec:ssh}; the account of it
shows whether the student's shell runs somewhere and whether files live
on machines.

