The sixth item is the open twin of the closed item \emph{which computer
answers} (\cref{app:knowledge-items}): the same situation---an [[ssh]],
a new prompt, an [[ls]]---but no alternatives, and a question that asks
only what is listed.
The word \emph{computer} is kept out of the stem on purpose: asking
\enquote{which computer's files} already tells the student that there
are two computers to choose between, and that the choice is the point,
which is precisely the aspect they are to discern for themselves
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.
An account that mentions the machine at all, and names the right one,
has discerned it unprompted; an account that describes \enquote{the
files in the folder} and stops has not.
The closed item, where the alternatives cannot help naming the
machines, shows whether the student picks the right one once the choice
is laid out before them, and comparing the two answers of one student
tells how much of the discernment the alternatives do: an account that
never mentions a machine, followed by the correct choice, is the
alternatives doing the work.
The item follows the general account of the remote shell so that its
pointed question does not steer that account, and its title avoids the
closed item's title as a substring, since the analysis
(\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) finds the report columns by title.

