\subsubsection{What the end quiz shows for an account}
\label{app:open-items-feedback}

The end quiz has an answer for the open items too.
Under each account it shows the ways of seeing we expect for that
stimulus, from the first impression to the one the module aims at---an
outcome space, ordered as phenomenography orders one: each level
discerns something the level before it does not
\autocite[Ch.~2]{NCOL}.
The levels are \emph{provisional}: no account has been read yet, so they
are posited from the candidate critical aspects of the chapter each item
belongs to
(\cref{sec:shell-aspects,sec:filesystem-aspects,sec:composition-aspects,sec:ssh-aspects})
and, for the terminal itself, from the outcome space of the one earlier
phenomenographic study of Unix---a set of commands, a tool for solving
problems, a working environment \autocite{DoyleLister2006Unix}---and
they are written for the student, in the words of the stem.
Three things keep them from contaminating the data.
They are shown only after submission, so the account is written first;
they are shown only in the end quiz, since the start quiz hides all
feedback (\cref{app:quiz}); and they are never given to the coder or to
the model that pre-codes the accounts
(\cref{app:quiz-analysis})---the categories are what the analysis is to
find, and a coder who had our expectation in front of them would be
confirming it.
Once the first cohort's accounts are coded, the empirical outcome space
replaces these lists, and the closed items' explanations are revisited
against the distractor frequencies.
