The platform shows such a text as the item's feedback; the first line
says what the list is, and the list runs from the least to the most
powerful way of seeing.

<<outcome space: what the terminal is>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Different answers to this question show different ways of seeing the terminal. In order, from a first impression to the way of seeing the module aims at:</p><ol><li>A black window for experts, where you type commands you have memorised and hope they work.</li><li>A place to type commands: each line names a program, and the text that comes back is the program&rsquo;s answer&mdash;or the shell saying it could not find or run it.</li><li>Another way into the same computer as the desktop: the shell reads the line as a sentence of a small language&mdash;a program and its arguments&mdash;runs the program on the same files and folders the desktop shows, and whatever comes back, output, silence or an error message, is information about what happened.</li></ol>"}
