\subsubsection{The command model}

The command line is a language, not a search box (\cref{sec:shell}): the
shell parses the line into a command and its arguments; it does not guess
what the words mean.
The item gives a line of ordinary English and asks what happens.
The correct answer reads the first word as the name of a program to run;
the search-box distractor treats the line as a query over the machine;
the remaining distractors credit the shell with understanding the words
or silently correcting them.
This item is the one the start quiz leaves out: there the same stem is
the open item \emph{what happens to this line} (\cref{app:open-items}),
because the alternatives spell out the command model---that the first
word names a program---to students who may be meeting a shell for the
first time, and the start quiz promises not to reveal what the course
will teach.
The end quiz carries both forms, so for this aspect the movement is read
from the account at the start to the item at the end, and the paired
per-item gain of \cref{app:quiz-analysis} is computed over the other
items only.
The item keeps position 11 in the end quiz; the start quiz simply has a
gap there.

