\subsubsection{Composition}

A file is not a program.
The source end of a pipe is a program's output, and a file enters a
pipeline only through a program that reads it; the pipe is not the file
at either end.
It is common in our course to see the file itself named on the left of
the pipe, as if naming it would send its contents on---a course
observation (\cref{sec:method-vt-analysis}) that this item tests.
The item shows that line and asks what happens.
The correct answer has the shell look for a program called
[[report.txt]] and fail; the leading distractor sends the file's
contents down the pipe; the others have [[wc]] count the name, or have
the pipe write the count back into the file.
The item comes before its partner, the working pipeline, on purpose:
shown after [[cat report.txt | wc -l]], the line with the file on the
left is wrong by inspection---the [[cat]] is visibly missing---and the
item would measure comparison rather than discernment.
Shown first, the student has to judge the line on its own.

