The pipe is not the file (\cref{sec:composition}): a pipe carries one
program's output straight into another program, rather than storing it.
Composite commands are exactly where the difficulty is documented---users
can know the component commands yet fail to compose them
\autocite{Doane1992UnixPrompts}---so the distractors are the plausible
wrong models of what the pipe does.
The item shows the working form of the previous item's line and asks
what is true of it.
The correct answer streams [[cat]]'s output into the reader; the leading
distractor treats the pipe as a file, saving the output under the second
program's name; the others reverse the order or deny the connection.

