<<feedback: where the remote file ends up>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>The program ran on the server, so the file it created is on the server, in the folder the shell stood in there; nothing copies it to the laptop, and closing the connection deletes nothing. To have it on the laptop, fetch it&mdash;with scp, for example.</p>"}
@

A shell runs somewhere: every prompt belongs to exactly one machine, and
a command acts on that machine (\cref{sec:ssh}).
After an [[ssh]] the prompt looks the same but the machine has changed,
which is precisely why it is easy to lose track.
The item asks what a listing shows after connecting to a server.
The stem does not say \emph{computer}---that would already tell the
student that the machine is the point, and a test of discernment must
not point out the aspect \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}; the alternatives
cannot avoid naming the machines, and that is the discerning they do for
the student.
The correct answer follows the shell to the server; the leading
distractor stays with the laptop the student is sitting at---the
undiscerned-aspect case of not knowing which machine the command acts
on.
Its open twin, \emph{what ls lists after ssh} (\cref{app:open-items}),
asks the same thing without alternatives; the two answers of one student
show how much of the discernment the alternatives do.

